<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taiping Heavenly Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1851 CE – 1864 CE · capital Tianjing (Nanjing)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Christian-inspired rebel state that ruled much of southern China from Nanjing for over a decade, in a civil war that cost perhaps twenty million lives.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan">Hong Xiuquan</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> who repeatedly failed the civil service examinations, came to believe from visions and Christian tracts that he was the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. His God Worshipping Society gathered followers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>, and in 1851 he proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> Heavenly Kingdom. His armies marched north and in 1853 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, renamed Tianjing.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> banned opium, foot-binding, idolatry and prostitution, segregated men and women in the early years and proposed common ownership of land, though this was hardly applied. Hong governed through 'kings' such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-xiuqing">Yang Xiuqing</a> until a bloody purge in 1856. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-rengan">Hong Rengan</a> later proposed Western-style railways and banks.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Provincial armies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-army">Xiang Army</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-army">Huai Army</a>, aided by Western-officered troops, gradually recovered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> valley. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan">Hong Xiuquan</a> died in 1864, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> fell weeks later. The war devastated the lower Yangtze and weakened the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> for good.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taiping-heavenly-kingdom.jpg" alt="Taiping Heavenly Kingdom" width="320" height="243"><figcaption>Image: M.Bitton · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taiping_Heavenly_Kingdom_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1851 CE – 1864 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tianjing (Nanjing)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hong Xiuquan, Yang Xiuqing, Hong Rengan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Taiping Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chinese (Hakka, Mandarin)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Taiping, Heavenly Kingdom of Great Peace</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1858&amp;focus=taiping-heavenly-kingdom" aria-label="Taiping Heavenly Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7736 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taiping Heavenly Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8056 1267L7567 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8056 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8056 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1858&amp;focus=taiping-heavenly-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
